Tell the keyboard what kind of field has focus.

The keyboard was never told about the field, so every field got the plain text keyboard with suggestions on, password fields included. The runtime now passes on what Flutter sends in TextInput.setClient, as Qt's input context does for native apps (Maliit's updateWidgetInformation):

- keyboardType number and phone show the number pad and the dial pad; emailAddress and url show the usual keys with "@" or "/" in place of the comma.
- obscureText fields are sent as hidden text, with no suggestions, corrections or capitals. visiblePassword gets the same, apart from hiding.
- enableSuggestions, autocorrect and textCapitalization turn suggestions, corrections and capitals at the start of sentences on and off.
- textInputAction sets the enter key's icon from the Sailfish theme: accept for done and go, and next, search and send.
- TextInputType.none keeps the keyboard hidden, as on Android.

The text around the cursor is sent too, without the word being composed, so the keyboard can capitalise the start of sentences.

